74
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Now, if you haven't already heard about it. Shelterbox is an international disaster relief organization

75
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:13,000
that's provided shelter and life-saving equipment after natural and other disasters since its founding in the year 2000..

76
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:20,000
What Shelterbox provides is unique and it doesn't really duplicate any other established relief organizations.

77
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:23,000
It's been quite effective and quite interesting.

78
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:32,000
It's based in the UK and Shelterbox USA is one of 20 Shelterbox affiliates around the world.

79
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
There's also more than 50 highly trained Shelterbox response teams members based here in the United States

80
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
who distribute the boxes in disaster zones and they work closely with local organizations.

81
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Last year, Shelterbox USA officially partnered with the Boy Scouts of America.

82
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:56,000
I want to welcome Mark Dwyer, who is a Shelterbox response team member,

83
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,000
and he also heads up the program for Shelterbox USA. How are you doing today, Mark?

84
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Good morning Clark.

85
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Shelterbox also has 300 volunteer ambassadors in the United States.

86
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,000
Now what these ambassadors do is they make contact with different service organizations and youth groups

87
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:18,000
and they explain the Shelterbox mission, and this helps build awareness of the organization

88
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:22,000
and helps raise funds to supply Shelterbox.

89
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
One of those ambassadors is Doug Matz.

90
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
Now you might know Doug from his blog.

91
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,000
He's a scout master in Troop 83 in Kenilworth, New Jersey.

92
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
How are you doing today, Doug?

93
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
I'm good. Thank you,

94
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Mark. let me turn to you first.

95
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:40,000
