96
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
What's your background in scouting?

97
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
You know I'm actually a lifelong scouter.

98
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
I'm an Eagle Scout, My son's an Eagle Scout.

99
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:55,000
I've been involved with Troop 82 here in the Chicago area for about eight years now.

100
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:00,000
I've got different roles within that. so really a lifetime of scouting.

101
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
So this is a pretty natural setup for you then

102
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,000
It is.

103
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:10,000
There's so many things between scouting and Shelterbox that are very similar:

104
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:17,000
All the life skills that you learn, the leadership and the citizenship in the world-

105
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
those types of skills that translate very directly to Shelterbox.

106
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Doug, you got involved with Shelterbox through a visit to the Jamboree last year.

107
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
That's correct.

108
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:35,000
My first exposure to Shelterbox was touring the Jamboree, the national Jamboree.

109
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,000
My son and I went out for a weekend road trip.

110
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:49,000
I got to see the setup and remembered being extremely impressed with both the quality of gear

111
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:53,000
that they were using as well as their overall mission.

112
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:58,000
The fact that they could pack everything into such a small box

113
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
was just anyone who's ever packed for a scout camp out.

114
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
That was kind of impressive to me as well.

115
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:08,000
At that point there were a lot of things to see at the Jamboree.

116
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
We went on and were touring other activities.

117
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:17,000
I guess it was March when the earthquake and such hit Japan

118
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
that the name Shelterbox kind of came across my radar again.

119
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:26,000
I've always had a soft spot in my heart for Japan.

120
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:32,000
It was at that point that I decided that I really need to do something more here.

121
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:36,000
As you know, Scouts is all about service to others.

122
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:40,000
I wanted to step forward and set that example for my Scouts

123
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
and get more involved.

124
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
To me, Shelterbox was the perfect fit for that.

125
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
I think that's a pretty common experience, Doug,

126
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:53,000
because we see these horrific things happen, these natural disasters

127
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and other kinds of disasters unfold around the world,

128
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
and we wonder: what in the world can we do?

129
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
How can we affect that?

130
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,000
How did you end up becoming an ambassador?

131
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
The day that the earthquake in Japan was announced,

132
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,000
I was all over the Shelterbox website like crazy.

133
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:17,000
I got in touch with the coordinator who works with all the volunteers

134
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
Registered right away, said

135
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
I want to be a volunteer eventually. if possible,

136
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
I would like to be on the response team.

137
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
What do you need? What can I do?

138
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
There was a brief interview process

139
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
and we worked through some deals.

140
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Since then I've been selling the Shelterbox mission

141
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
anywhere and everywhere I can.

142
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Mark, maybe you can tell us, as a member of the response team,

143
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
what are we talking about here?

144
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
We're talking about a literal box of stuff, right?

145
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Right, I mean literally. picture a large rubber-made container

146
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:57,000
and in that is what most scouters would relate to as a patrol box.

147
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,000
It's got a 10-person tent, It's got blankets,

148
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:04,000
It's got stove ground sheets,

149
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,000
There's a toolkit so people have hammer saws,

150
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
things they use to rebuild their life.

151
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
There's an activity kit for the kids.

152
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
It is literally everything that a group of 10 needs to live

153
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
out in the field.

154
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Mark, tell me how this all got started.

155
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Who came up with the idea and how did Shelterbox get started?

156
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Right, Shelterbox was started in the year 2000..

157
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
It was really the vision of one gentleman,

158
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
a guy named Tom Henderson.

159
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
He's English and it's really a simple concept.

160
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
He was sitting at home watching the news one night,

161
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:39,000
like we all do, and he saw after a disaster.

162
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
he saw a bunch of people huddled under a tarp

163
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and a truck pulled up and they started throwing loaves of bread

164
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Off the truck and it was raining

165
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
and people were scrambling around for the food

166
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and he just said: you know, this is so wrong,

167
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
Why are they living like this

168
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,000
Kind of thought about. it went home, said: you know,

169
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
if I lost my home I'd go in the garage,

170
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
I'd pull out my camping equipment,

171
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
we'd set it up in the backyard and we'd be fine.

172
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
So why can't we do that for people?

173
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
You know did some research, got involved,

174
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:10,000
started looking at it and realized nobody was doing that

175
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and really took it to his rotary club

176
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
and started packing the first boxes out of his garage

177
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
in the year 2000,, 2001..

178
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:22,000
And since then we've now been to more than 160 disasters

179
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
in more than 70 countries.

180
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:28,000
So it's really pretty amazing how much it's grown in 11 years.

181
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Wow, And this is something-

182
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
the way that this kit was developed over time,

183
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
I guess, was testing things.

184
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Yeah, it's evolved.

185
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
I mean the equipment has evolved over the years.

186
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:44,000
the tents specifically. you know

187
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,000
we're probably on about our sixth or seventh evolution of the tent.

188
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
You know it's pretty difficult if you think about it.

189
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
We're going into every potential climate in the world.

190
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:00,000
We're going into every different location

191
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
during all different weather conditions.

192
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
So it has to be a good, you know, four season tent.

193
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,000
It has to hold up very well the UV rays.

194
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
So you know we're always tweaking it

195
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
and improving it to that level.

196
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Our response team members in many cases

197
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
live in the tents while they're in the field.

198
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
So if there's any issues, problems whatever,

199
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
we're firsthand using them

200
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and coming back and working to fix those problems.

201
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,000
So give me an idea of what a response team does

202
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
and how this works. once it hits the ground,

203
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
Sure, When there's a disaster,

204
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
usually our response teams are deployed within

205
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
24 to 48 hours,

206
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So we have people standing by ready to go.

207
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
They then get into that country,

208
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:48,000
like I said, within 24 to 48 hours.

209
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:51,000
They're doing a quick assessment of the disaster.

210
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Certain disasters we know immediately

211
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
are going to be huge scale,

212
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
things like Haiti or Japan-

213
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
and we start shipping gear right away.

214
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
But generally, as our teams land,

215
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
they're doing a quick assessment

216
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
of getting up into the disaster area,

217
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
seeing how bad it is

218
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
and then calling for aid as it comes over.

219
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
So basically, we're then shipping boxes in

220
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:18,000
chartering planes, flying it into the disaster area

221
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and the response teams then shepherd that all the way through.

222
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So they're meeting planes at the airport,

223
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
making sure stuff gets through customs.

224
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:31,000
In many cases, that airport that they're flying into

225
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,000
can be hours away from the actual disaster zone,

226
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:39,000
So they'll arrange transportation to move it up into the disaster area

227
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
and then work with locals to distribute.

228
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:46,000
We've moved boxes on tons of different ways of transport.

229
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
Obviously the most common would be trucks,

230
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
but we've moved them on boats,

231
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
We've moved them in helicopters,

232
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
We've moved them on burrows,

233
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
We've even moved them on camels in the Middle East.

234
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
You've been directly involved as a relief team member

235
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
in the field, have you?

236
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
I have, yeah,

237
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
I've been a relief team member for about three years.

238
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
I've been on about seven deployments.

239
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:13,000
I've been to Somaliland, which is northern Somalia.

240
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:18,000
I've been to Niger, Haiti twice, Colombia, Japan

241
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,000
And then my latest deployment was actually into the southern United States

242
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:26,000
after the tornadoes that came through Tuscaloosa and Joplin.

243
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:29,000
We actually distributed aid into northwest Arkansas

244
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
that was also hit by those storms.

245
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Were scouts involved in that at all?

246
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:37,000
Actually, I've been fortunate enough to work with scouts

247
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
on two different deployments.

248
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Right after the Japanese tsunami,

249
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
I worked with scout groups in the Tori, Japan.

250
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
We had about 100 scouts come up and help set up tents.

251
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
And then in northwest Arkansas,

252
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:53,000
around the towns of Altus and Denning,

253
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:57,000
we had about 45 both-boy scouts and girls scouts come out

254
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:01,000
and help us set up tents and equipment into those areas.

255
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
It's great working with scouts in the field

256
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
because they understand camping. they understand what it is.

257
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
We pretty much have to show them once how to set up a tent

258
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
and they get it and they jump right in and help us out.

259
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
There's a pretty solid example right here in our own backyard.

260
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:21,000
I'm really impressed with the practicality.

261
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:24,000
It's interesting because a lot of people

262
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
just don't really think about shelter.

263
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
It's critical after a disaster,

264
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
depending on what the environmental conditions are,

265
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
But it's also giving people some dignity too

266
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:37,000
that they're not just living under a blue tarp,

267
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
sleeping in the mud and on the rain,

268
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:43,000
have a place to have some privacy for their family.

269
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,000
The other thing that's pretty amazing is

270
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
we raise awareness and raise funds for it,

271
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
so all of the equipment is literally funded

272
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
before a disaster happens.

273
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:57,000
So we have boxes of them prepared

274
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
or ready to go out and when a disaster hits

275
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
we can be shipping those immediately.

276
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
It's not like the disaster hit yesterday

277
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and we start raising funds or money the next couple of days

278
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
We've actually already raised that money

279
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and have the equipment ready to go,

280
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
so the pipeline's full and ready to keep going

281
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
Folks. this does cost money

282
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:20,000
and that's the one way for all of us to be involved in it.

283
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Doug, tell me a little bit about what a shelter box costs

284
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:28,000
and how they get funded from the ambassador standpoint.

285
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Sure,

286
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
What we do basically is we'll go around to different groups

287
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:35,000
and do presentations promoting shelter box.

288
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:37,000
That can be rotary clubs,

289
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
It can be other civic groups.

290
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
I've had the opportunity of actually giving presentations

291
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
to a number of scout units.

292
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Not every presentation- from my perspective,

293
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:51,000
as a lot of them are scout targeted-

294
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:53,000
is fundraising based,

295
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
As some of those, like the scout ones,

296
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:58,000
were not always looking for scouts to raise money,

297
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
though. I have been involved in one project

298
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
that did pretty phenomenally there.

299
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
In some cases it's just about spreading awareness

300
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:09,000
of shelter box and teaching them how their scout skills

301
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:14,000
really have practicality in this type of disaster situation.

302
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Yeah, it's really something they can get their hands on,

303
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
that they can understand.

304
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:23,000
Like I said before, it's just so amazingly practical.

305
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:28,000
What does it cost to put a shelter box together?

306
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,000
The going cost for a complete kit is $1,000 per box.

307
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:37,000
That $1,000 covers not only the contents of the box

308
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:40,000
but it also covers the storage,

309
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
the transportation and logistics.

310
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
As Mark said, within 48 hours

311
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:48,000
we're getting those boxes on the ground.

312
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,000
We're shipping and transportation charges.

313
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
It also covers towards getting the response team members there

314
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:57,000
with the boxes, getting them in country

315
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
to accompany the deployments

316
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Mark. I did a little checking up just out of curiosity

317
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
about the organization itself.

318
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:10,000
It's a very highly rated 501c3,

319
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:13,000
and that rating is based on the fact that the money

320
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:16,000
that comes in- the vast majority of the money

321
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:21,000
that goes in- goes directly to the core mission

322
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:25,000
of creating these boxes and getting them where they need to go.

323
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:29,000
When I say vast, it's like 93% or something like that.

324
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:30,000
Correct, yeah,

325
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,000
I mean, shelter box is really an amazing organization

326
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:36,000
because of the amount of volunteers that we have involved.

327
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:39,000
Everything from our response teams are volunteers.

328
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
Our ambassadors are volunteers.

329
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:45,000
We have a very, very minimal staff here in the US

330
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:49,000
that really just supports the volunteer effort.

331
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Generally, people want to help.

332
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:55,000
especially after a disaster where they see these images on TV.

333
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:58,000
They want to know what they can do to get involved and help.

334
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:01,000
We're very fortunate to have these many amount of volunteers

335
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
out there willing to help us.

336
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,000
We're very familiar with the medical teams that go out,

337
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
the search and rescue teams and everything like that,

338
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:12,000
but we don't often see what happens to the victims

339
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:16,000
of these disasters two weeks later or three weeks later,

340
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
what their lives are like and what they're doing.

341
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:21,000
Our media is very event-driven:

342
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
because it's a headline news today

343
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:27,000
It doesn't mean there's a follow-up further on down the line,

344
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:30,000
but it doesn't stop the suffering

345
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
You're basically.

346
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that's why we try and get in that very first wave

347
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,000
of relief that comes in.

348
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:41,000
In Japan, we had people on the ground within 24 hours. in Haiti,

349
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
We had people on the ground within 24 hours.

350
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:46,000
Our first need when we got in to Haiti

351
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
was we were actually using tents as field hospitals.

352
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
Our guys on the ground determined

353
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
that that was the biggest need at the moment,

354
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
so they took our first tents as they were arriving

355
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
and set those up and had

356
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
using them not only for operating theaters

357
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
but also for recovery rooms.

358
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,000
In Haiti alone, Shelter Box provided shelter

359
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
for over 300,000 people-

360
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
an amazing effort for a charity

361
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
that most people have never really heard of.

362
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
I guess we really don't imagine

363
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:16,000
what it would be like to, you know,

364
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:19,000
be on the other side of one of these events

365
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
and not have a roof over your head,

366
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
not have a way to provide water for your family,

367
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
not have a way to cook food,

368
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:28,000
not even have any of the basic tools

369
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
that you take for granted every day.

370
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
Right,

371
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
We're on the response team.

372
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
We see all that.

373
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:39,000
We see people in almost a state of shock.

374
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
You know, in many cases

375
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:44,000
they're just trying to account for their family members,

376
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
make sure everybody's together,

377
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
trying to locate other relatives,

378
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
other family members.

379
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
It is quite confusing.

380
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
It is quite chaotic.

381
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
So you know,

382
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
a place to live, a place to call your home,

383
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
a place to put your things,

384
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
does provide a little,

385
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
a huge amount of stability to the regions

386
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
during a disaster.

387
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
You also mentioned the issue of dignity.

388
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
Imagine 10,000 people

389
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
living in an area,

390
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
packed in together,

391
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
literally your family,

392
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
within a 10 by 10 area,

393
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
and you have to do everything

394
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
within that 10 by 10 area.

395
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
So you're changing your clothes,

396
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
you're getting dressed, you're sleeping.

397
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
You know, if you're doing that

398
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
out in the open under a blue tarp,

399
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
personal dignity's gone.

400
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
There's no

401
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
ability for you to

402
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
have that dignity.

403
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
So that's a very important part of our mission

404
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
is to give people that self-dignity.

405
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
Doug, when you go out and speak to groups and things,

406
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
what are the most common questions that you get asked?

407
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
They'll ask a lot of questions.

408
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
as far as

409
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
what the response team

410
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
responsibilities are.

411
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
They'll ask about things on. you know

412
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
how they get them through customs.

413
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
They will ask about, like what you touched on,

414
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
whether this is actually going. you know

415
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
the fundraising is going towards boxes.

416
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
how much of this is really? you know

417
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
administration and overhead.

418
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
You know we can speak to that quite confidently.

419
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
You know they'll ask

420
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
a lot of technical

421
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
questions in fact about the actual

422
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
equipment that we're

423
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
deploying out into the field.

424
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
So when I do my demonstrations

425
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
I've got a demo kit from

426
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
Shelter Box. so if space permits

427
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
we'll set up the tent.

428
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
We'll let you know whoever we're presenting to

429
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
do a walkthrough of the tent.

430
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
They can actually see the equipment

431
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
that's going out there. and

432
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
you know, probably one of the things

433
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
that's most impressive is they'll

434
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
see me show up with one of these green boxes

435
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
and be like,

436
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
oh, that's it,

437
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
Where's the rest? And then it's,

438
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
you know, almost like the clown car,

439
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
all the things that keep coming out

440
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
and coming out.

441
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
You know, literally, other than

442
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
food and medicine, it's

443
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
everything for an extended family

444
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
of ten in one condensed box.

445
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
We're all scouters here

446
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
and we know that we have this kind

447
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
of magpie-like reaction

448
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
to shiny objects that we can take camping

449
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
And it does kind of appeal

450
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
to that. I mean it's a pretty impressive kit.

451
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
Yeah, it absolutely is.

452
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
I mean it's been

453
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
described a number of times almost as

454
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
the ultimate patrol box,

455
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
because you've got everything from your

456
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
tent and shelter, you've got stoves,

457
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
you've got blankets-

458
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
If the environment calls for it,

459
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
mosquito nets are provided,

460
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
water purification

461
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
tools- it could be ideal

462
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
for a camp-out situation.

463
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
You've got everything except your food

464
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
and your firewood. These are wood-burning stoves

465
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
that are in it. There are two different models

466
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
that go out.

467
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
There is one that handles

468
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
basically any type of liquid

469
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
fuel and then there's

470
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
a separate pioneer

471
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
stove model that basically anything

472
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
that's solid that burns

473
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
can fuel that stove for cooking.

474
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
Mark, anything you wanted to add about

475
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
the contents of the box.

476
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
The only other thing I'd like to add is

477
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
when a group does sponsor a box-

478
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
a group or an individual- they can

479
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
actually track that box and follow

480
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
where in the world it goes.

481
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
Oh, really, huh.

482
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
When they send in their donation,

483
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
they get another letter back saying:

484
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
great, thank you for your donation,

485
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
You just sponsored box number

486
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
1234..

487
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
That box now gets sent to a disaster somewhere

488
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
in the world. They get another letter back

489
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
saying: great, your box number

490
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
1234 just went to

491
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
the flooding in Columbia

492
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
or the earthquake in Haiti

493
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
or the earthquake in Japan

494
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
or the drought in

495
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
the Horn of Africa right now.

496
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
So it's really pretty

497
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
helpful to know exactly where their money

498
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
went, exactly where the box they

499
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
funded went. They can then go on our

500
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
website. they can look at pictures

501
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
that our response team members take

502
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
there of basically the conditions

503
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
that people are living in. How does one

504
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
become a response team member?

505
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
The response team member is a pretty

506
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
involved process.

507
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
It took me about a year

508
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
from start to finish, from the time I

509
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
applied till I actually got my SRT

510
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
designation,

511
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
The first one being background checks,

